    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Brings Fresh Adventure-Ready Concepts at the 2026 SEMA Show Under the Woodland Banner\nHonda CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports Is a Tongue Twister, Weighs Less but Offers More\n82 photos\nPhoto: Honda\nI personally hate the way motorcycle makers from around the world name their products. Instead of going for straight-up names like carmakers do, they throw everything into the bike's name: their class, engine size, model family, and even the segment they play in. And that's how we end up with motorcycles named something on the line of CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports.\nThe name probably does not sound familiar, despite some of its component parts having been around for a while now. That's understandable, seeing how the Japanese from Honda introduced it for the first time this week at the EICMA motorcycle show in Milan, Italy.\nThe all-new model, as it's described, joins the bike maker's adventure offering as yet another version of the Africa Twin, really. I mean, it borrows everything of importance from that two-wheeler, including the frame, swingarm, and engine. Why, even the riding position is the same.\nWhat the Enduro Sports brings new to the table is a lighter body (20 pounds/nine kg less than the base bike), more ground clearance, a taller seat height, and a dedicated suspension system.\nOh, and it also tampers a bit with the components it borrowed from the two-wheeler it is based on, so that all-new bike with increased off-road performance phrase Yamaha uses to describe the new model makes a lot of sense.\nThe Enduro Sports uses Africa Twin's steel semi-double cradle frame and its aluminum swingarm, modified to cut weight a bit, but also to better support the 1,131cc parallel twin-cylinder engine.\nMore ground clearance for the ride (which would be 260 mm) was achieved by using a purpose-built suspension system. It comprises 49 mm Showa closed-cartridge USD forks at the front and a shock of the same make at the rear.\nCompared to the engine deployed on the other Africa Twin models, the one on the Enduro Sports is significantly more powerful, with the numbers reading 117 hp and 119 Nm of torque.\nThat's 16 and seven Nm more, which were gained, not in small part, on account of the powerplant being slightly larger than the version that was used until the 2026 model year (1,084cc).\nThe powerplant works together with a six-speed manual transmission, but there will be the option for riders to select the six-speed Dual Clutch Transmission (DCT). A redesigned intake system is on deck regardless of that choice.\nThe new Honda bike moves on spoked wheels sized 21 inches at the front and 18 inches at the rear, both of them shod in Metzeler Karoo Street as standard, or Michelin Anakee Wild as an option.\nHonda does not say exactly when the new Africa Twin model will become available, or how much it will cost, but I can tell you for a fact that this one is not the only tongue twister in the bike maker's stable. And I will give you a single example from the refreshed range that was announced at EICMA: CRF1150L Africa Twin Adventure Sports Honda Sensing Ride.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHonda Honda CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports honda africa twin EICMA 2026 2026 eicma\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT They Bought a Lexus LC for $123,494, Put It Up for Sale Immediately, Refused $163,500",
